## ECS Service Auto Scaling

### Overview

ECS Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of running tasks (containers) based on CloudWatch metrics.

### Prerequisites

Create an IAM service-linked role for Application Auto Scaling:

```bash
aws iam create-service-linked-role \
  --aws-service-name ecs.application-autoscaling.amazonaws.com
```

---

### Step 1: Create ECS Cluster and Service

Create an ECS cluster:

```bash
aws ecs create-cluster --cluster-name my-ecs-cluster
```

Register a task definition:

```bash
aws ecs register-task-definition \
  --family my-api-task \
  --network-mode awsvpc \
  --requires-compatibilities FARGATE \
  --cpu 256 \
  --memory 512 \
  --container-definitions '[
    {
      "name": "my-api-container",
      "image": "123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-api:latest",
      "portMappings": [
        {
          "containerPort": 80,
          "hostPort": 80,
          "protocol": "tcp"
        }
      ],
      "logConfiguration": {
        "logDriver": "awslogs",
        "options": {
          "awslogs-group": "/ecs/my-api",
          "awslogs-region": "us-east-1",
          "awslogs-stream-prefix": "ecs"
        }
      }
    }
  ]'
```

Create an ECS service:

```bash
aws ecs create-service \
  --cluster my-ecs-cluster \
  --service-name my-api-service \
  --task-definition my-api-task:1 \
  --desired-count 2 \
  --launch-type FARGATE \
  --network-configuration 'awsvpcConfiguration={
    subnets=[subnet-12345678,subnet-87654321],
    securityGroups=[sg-0123456789abcdef0],
    assignPublicIp=ENABLED
  }' \
  --load-balancers 'targetGroupArn=arn:aws:elasticloadbalancing:us-east-1:123456789012:targetgroup/my-tg/1234567890abcdef,containerName=my-api-container,containerPort=80'
```

---

### Step 2: Register Scalable Target

Register the ECS service as a scalable target:

```bash
aws application-autoscaling register-scalable-target \
  --service-namespace ecs \
  --resource-id service/my-ecs-cluster/my-api-service \
  --scalable-dimension ecs:service:DesiredCount \
  --min-capacity 2 \
  --max-capacity 10
```

---

### Step 3: Create Target Tracking Scaling Policy

Scale tasks to maintain 50% CPU utilization:

```bash
aws application-autoscaling put-scaling-policy \
  --policy-name ecs-cpu-scaling \
  --service-namespace ecs \
  --resource-id service/my-ecs-cluster/my-api-service \
  --scalable-dimension ecs:service:DesiredCount \
  --policy-type TargetTrackingScaling \
  --target-tracking-scaling-policy-configuration '{
    "TargetValue": 50.0,
    "PredefinedMetricSpecification": {
      "PredefinedMetricType": "ECSServiceAverageCPUUtilization"
    },
    "ScaleOutCooldown": 60,
    "ScaleInCooldown": 300
  }'
```

---

### Step 4: Create Memory Scaling Policy

Scale based on memory utilization:

```bash
aws application-autoscaling put-scaling-policy \
  --policy-name ecs-memory-scaling \
  --service-namespace ecs \
  --resource-id service/my-ecs-cluster/my-api-service \
  --scalable-dimension ecs:service:DesiredCount \
  --policy-type TargetTrackingScaling \
  --target-tracking-scaling-policy-configuration '{
    "TargetValue": 75.0,
    "PredefinedMetricSpecification": {
      "PredefinedMetricType": "ECSServiceAverageMemoryUtilization"
    },
    "ScaleOutCooldown": 60,
    "ScaleInCooldown": 300
  }'
```

---

### Step 5: Monitor ECS Scaling

View ECS service status:

```bash
# Describe service
aws ecs describe-services \
  --cluster my-ecs-cluster \
  --services my-api-service

# List running tasks
aws ecs list-tasks \
  --cluster my-ecs-cluster \
  --service-name my-api-service

# Describe tasks
aws ecs describe-tasks \
  --cluster my-ecs-cluster \
  --tasks $(aws ecs list-tasks --cluster my-ecs-cluster --query taskArns --output text)
```

