## Best Practices

### 1. Set Appropriate Target Values
- **CPU:** 50-70% (leaves room for spikes)
- **Memory:** 60-80% (memory is harder to predict)
- **Request Count:** Scale based on your app's capacity per instance

### 2. Configure Cooldown Periods
```bash
Scale-Out Cooldown: 60-120 seconds
  (Respond quickly to traffic spikes)

Scale-In Cooldown: 300-600 seconds
  (Avoid rapid scale-down)
```

### 3. Use Multiple Scaling Policies
Combine policies for better coverage:
- Target tracking for base scaling
- Step scaling for aggressive responses
- Scheduled scaling for predictable patterns

### 4. Monitor and Alert
```bash
aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm \
  --alarm-name asg-at-max-capacity \
  --metric-name GroupDesiredCapacity \
  --namespace AWS/AutoScaling \
  --statistic Average \
  --period 300 \
  --threshold 10 \
  --comparison-operator GreaterThanOrEqualToThreshold \
  --alarm-actions arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:123456789012:notifications
```

### 5. Use Health Checks
Enable ELB health checks:
```bash
aws autoscaling put-lifecycle-hook \
  --auto-scaling-group-name WebAppASG \
  --lifecycle-hook-name draining-hook \
  --lifecycle-transition autoscaling:EC2_INSTANCE_TERMINATING \
  --default-result CONTINUE \
  --heartbeat-timeout 300
```

### 6. Test Scaling Behavior
- Stress test your application
- Verify scaling triggers correctly
- Ensure graceful shutdown of instances/tasks
- Monitor logs during scaling events

